1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:11,691
This is MATH 239, video 8. The topic is the generating series of a set with a weight function. This is Section 2.2.1 of the course notes.

2
00:00:12,141 --> 00:00:18,071
After this video, you can say when a function is a weight function, and why it must be one.

3
00:00:18,521 --> 00:00:23,065
You can write the generating series of a set, and read its coefficients.

4
00:00:23,515 --> 00:00:31,302
You can prove Proposition 2.7: the coefficient of x to the n is the number of objects of weight n.

5
00:00:31,752 --> 00:00:39,880
And you can solve the practice, tutorial and assignment questions of this topic, and two questions of the practice midterm.

6
00:00:40,330 --> 00:00:46,943
Here is a counting problem with many answers. Count the subsets of 1, 2, 3 of each size.

7
00:00:47,393 --> 00:00:57,825
Each row of three cells is one subset. A blue cell is an element of the subset. For example, the subset 1, 3 has blue cells 1 and 3.

8
00:00:58,275 --> 00:01:00,921
The subsets stand in the column of their size.

9
00:01:01,371 --> 00:01:05,424
The column heights are the counts. One subset has size 0.

10
00:01:05,874 --> 00:01:07,858
Three subsets have size 1.

11
00:01:08,308 --> 00:01:10,249
Three subsets have size 2.

12
00:01:10,699 --> 00:01:12,897
And one subset has size 3.

13
00:01:13,347 --> 00:01:23,373
Now write one term x to the size for each subset, and add the terms. The result is 1 plus 3 x plus 3 x squared plus x cubed.

14
00:01:23,823 --> 00:01:32,527
The coefficient of x to the n is the number of subsets of size n. For example, the coefficient of x squared is 3.

15
00:01:32,977 --> 00:01:35,836
So one power series holds all the counts.

16
00:01:36,286 --> 00:01:52,030
Section 2.1 did this for the subsets of 1 to n. The sum is 1 plus x, to the n. That is the Binomial Theorem. This video makes the idea general: any set, and any rule for the size of an object.

17
00:01:52,480 --> 00:02:00,992
Here are the coefficients of 1 plus x, cubed, as a row of cells. Cell n holds the coefficient of x to the n.

18
00:02:01,442 --> 00:02:09,954
The cells are 1, 3, 3, 1, and then zeros. These are the counts of the subsets of 1, 2, 3 by size.

19
00:02:10,404 --> 00:02:16,399
The Binomial Theorem gives these coefficients: n choose k, with n equal to 3.

20
00:02:16,849 --> 00:02:20,134
The second row is 1 over 1 minus x, cubed.

21
00:02:20,584 --> 00:02:25,405
Its coefficients count the multisets with 3 types of elements, by size.

22
00:02:25,855 --> 00:02:32,255
The Binomial Series gives the coefficient: n plus t minus 1, choose t minus 1.

23
00:02:32,705 --> 00:02:42,305
Look at x squared. A multiset with 3 types is a vector m 1, m 2, m 3 of natural numbers. Its size is the sum.

24
00:02:42,755 --> 00:02:45,977
There are 6 vectors with sum 2. Here they are.

25
00:02:46,427 --> 00:02:58,715
The formula gives 2 plus 3 minus 1, choose 3 minus 1. That is 4 choose 2, which is 6. So in both rows, a coefficient counts objects of one size.

26
00:02:59,165 --> 00:03:05,223
In general, we want a sequence of numbers g 0, g 1, g 2, and so on.

27
00:03:05,673 --> 00:03:09,855
For example, g n can be the number of objects of size n.

28
00:03:10,305 --> 00:03:14,273
We put g n in front of x to the n, and add the terms.

29
00:03:14,723 --> 00:03:19,501
The result G of x is the generating series of the sequence g.

30
00:03:19,951 --> 00:03:25,754
Here x is an indeterminate. It has no value. We never put a number in for x.

31
00:03:26,204 --> 00:03:33,649
The power of x only marks the position of each coefficient. For example, x cubed marks g 3.

32
00:03:34,099 --> 00:03:40,905
So x keeps the numbers g n apart. Chapter 4 shows how to get the numbers back out of the series.

33
00:03:41,355 --> 00:03:50,443
Now the definitions. Take a set S of objects. A function w from S to the natural numbers gives each object a weight.

34
00:03:50,893 --> 00:03:53,965
S n is the set of objects of weight n.

35
00:03:54,415 --> 00:03:58,937
w is a weight function if, for every n, the set S n is finite.

36
00:03:59,387 --> 00:04:03,953
The notes write script A and omega. The course uses S and w.

37
00:04:04,403 --> 00:04:09,800
Example. Let S be the set of all binary strings, and let the weight be the length.

38
00:04:10,250 --> 00:04:12,981
The empty string epsilon has length 0.

39
00:04:13,431 --> 00:04:16,012
The strings 0 and 1 have length 1.

40
00:04:16,462 --> 00:04:18,659
There are 4 strings of length 2.

41
00:04:19,109 --> 00:04:21,541
And there are 8 strings of length 3.

42
00:04:21,991 --> 00:04:24,210
The counts are 1, 2, 4, 8.

43
00:04:24,660 --> 00:04:33,300
In general, S n has 2 to the n strings. Each S n is finite, so the length is a weight function on the binary strings.

44
00:04:33,750 --> 00:04:43,777
Every object has exactly one weight. So S is the union of S 0, S 1, S 2, and so on, and these sets do not overlap.

45
00:04:44,227 --> 00:04:49,091
A countable union of finite sets is finite or countably infinite.

46
00:04:49,541 --> 00:05:01,722
The finite condition can fail. Practice problem P2-4 part b: all binary strings, and the weight v is twice the number of 0s. Pause the video and try it.

47
00:05:02,172 --> 00:05:09,127
A string with no 0s has weight 0. The empty string, 1, and 1 1 all have weight 0.

48
00:05:09,577 --> 00:05:16,297
So do 1 1 1, 1 1 1 1, and every string of only 1s. There is one for every length.

49
00:05:16,747 --> 00:05:21,333
So the set of weight 0 is infinite, and v is not a weight function.

50
00:05:21,783 --> 00:05:27,693
The set of weight 2 is also infinite: strings with one 0 and any number of 1s.

51
00:05:28,143 --> 00:05:40,367
Tutorial 2, question 1 part b has the same problem. Take tuples with entries 1, 2, 3, of any length. The weight counts the places where 1 is followed by 1.

52
00:05:40,817 --> 00:05:47,622
Every tuple of only 2s has weight 0. So again infinitely many tuples have weight 0.

53
00:05:48,072 --> 00:05:57,800
Why must each S n be finite? The coefficient of x to the n will be the number of objects of weight n. That must be a natural number.

54
00:05:58,250 --> 00:06:05,397
Infinitely many terms x to the 0 do not give a coefficient. So the sum is not a power series.

55
00:06:05,847 --> 00:06:19,436
Definition 2.6. The generating series of S with respect to w is phi S w of x. It is the sum, over all sigma in S, of x to the w of sigma.

56
00:06:19,886 --> 00:06:24,025
There is one term for each object. The exponent is its weight.

57
00:06:24,475 --> 00:06:31,045
Write phi S of x when w is clear. The notes also write A of x for a set script A.

58
00:06:31,495 --> 00:06:39,346
Example. Let S be the set of the 8 subsets of 1, 2, 3, and let the weight of sigma be the size of sigma.

59
00:06:39,796 --> 00:06:41,759
The empty set has weight 0.

60
00:06:42,209 --> 00:06:45,110
The three sets with one element have weight 1.

61
00:06:45,560 --> 00:06:48,355
The three sets with two elements have weight 2.

62
00:06:48,805 --> 00:06:51,343
The set 1, 2, 3 has weight 3.

63
00:06:51,793 --> 00:06:58,023
Each object gives one term: x to its weight. So the empty set gives x to the 0.

64
00:06:58,473 --> 00:07:00,841
And the set 1, 2 gives x squared.

65
00:07:01,291 --> 00:07:05,408
Add the 8 terms. This sum is the definition, written out.

66
00:07:05,858 --> 00:07:14,157
Collect the terms with equal powers. Phi S of x equals 1 plus 3 x plus 3 x squared plus x cubed.

67
00:07:14,607 --> 00:07:24,932
Check: put x equal to 1. Each term becomes 1, so the result is the number of objects, 8. And 1 plus 3 plus 3 plus 1 is 8.

68
00:07:25,382 --> 00:07:32,230
Now keep the same 8 subsets, but change the weight. The weight of sigma is now the sum of its elements.

69
00:07:32,680 --> 00:07:37,437
Put each subset in the column of its sum. The empty set has sum 0.

70
00:07:37,887 --> 00:07:41,386
The set 3 and the set 1, 2 both have sum 3.

71
00:07:41,836 --> 00:07:46,359
The set 1, 2, 3 has sum 1 plus 2 plus 3, which is 6.

72
00:07:46,809 --> 00:07:56,473
Count each column. The series is 1 plus x plus x squared plus 2 x cubed plus x to the 4 plus x to the 5 plus x to the 6.

73
00:07:56,923 --> 00:07:59,973
The set is the same, but the series is different.

74
00:08:00,423 --> 00:08:03,431
At x equal to 1, both series give 8.

75
00:08:03,881 --> 00:08:10,196
So the generating series depends on the set and on the weight. In a solution, always state both.

76
00:08:10,646 --> 00:08:20,459
Proposition 2.7 says why this works. If phi A of x has coefficients a n, then a n is the number of objects of weight n.

77
00:08:20,909 --> 00:08:27,267
The proof starts with the definition: one term x to the w of alpha, for each alpha in A.

78
00:08:27,717 --> 00:08:32,303
The chips are the terms of the example on the last slide, in any order.

79
00:08:32,753 --> 00:08:40,711
Group the objects by weight. Every object has exactly one weight n, so each term goes into exactly one group.

80
00:08:41,161 --> 00:08:42,825
The groups are the columns.

81
00:08:43,275 --> 00:08:58,208
In group n, every term is x to the n. So x to the n comes out of the inner sum. What is left is a sum of 1s, one for each object of weight n. For example, column 3 holds x cubed twice.

82
00:08:58,658 --> 00:09:07,319
A sum of 1s over A n is the size of A n. So the coefficient of x to the n is the number of objects of weight n.

83
00:09:07,769 --> 00:09:15,855
Each A n is finite, so each coefficient is a natural number. This is where the weight function condition is used.

84
00:09:16,305 --> 00:09:26,779
So a generating series is a list of counts. Count the objects of weight n, or read the coefficient of x to the n. The two give the same number.

85
00:09:27,229 --> 00:09:40,157
We need a short name for one coefficient. Definition 2.8: the coefficient of x to the k in G of x is written as x to the k, in square brackets, then G of x.

86
00:09:40,607 --> 00:09:44,511
For 1 over 1 minus x, every coefficient is 1.

87
00:09:44,961 --> 00:09:55,799
Example 2.9 uses the Binomial Series with t equal to 1 plus b. The coefficient of x to the n is n plus b, choose b.

88
00:09:56,249 --> 00:10:00,089
So the coefficient of x to the a is a plus b, choose b.

89
00:10:00,539 --> 00:10:07,451
For example, take 1 over 1 minus x, to the 4. Then 1 plus b is 4, so b is 3.

90
00:10:07,901 --> 00:10:17,885
For a equal to 2, the coefficient is 2 plus 3 choose 3. That is 5 choose 3, which is 10. The row shows 10 in the x squared cell.

91
00:10:18,335 --> 00:10:29,471
With this notation, Proposition 2.7 is one line. The coefficient of x to the n in phi S of x is the number of objects of weight n.

92
00:10:29,921 --> 00:10:33,547
Here is the method for a small set. List the objects.

93
00:10:33,997 --> 00:10:36,045
Find the weight of each object.

94
00:10:36,495 --> 00:10:39,461
Write one term x to the weight for each object.

95
00:10:39,911 --> 00:10:41,703
Collect the equal powers.

96
00:10:42,153 --> 00:10:46,910
Then check: at x equal to 1, the sum must be the number of objects.

97
00:10:47,360 --> 00:10:53,824
For a small set, sorting the objects into columns by weight is the same as collecting terms.

98
00:10:54,274 --> 00:11:09,143
For a large or infinite set, you cannot list every object. Then find the count of objects of weight n for a general n, and use it as the coefficient of x to the n. The next questions use both ways.

99
00:11:09,593 --> 00:11:24,847
Practice problem P1-8. S is the set of tuples of length 5 with exactly two 1s. The weight is the number of 0s between the two 1s. Find the generating series. Pause the video and try it.

100
00:11:25,297 --> 00:11:28,390
There are 5 choose 2, which is 10, tuples.

101
00:11:28,840 --> 00:11:33,341
Mark the 0s between the 1s in orange. Their number is the weight.

102
00:11:33,791 --> 00:11:39,978
For example, 1 0 0 0 1 has three orange 0s, so its weight is 3.

103
00:11:40,428 --> 00:11:45,441
Sort the tuples by weight. Weight 0 means the two 1s are next to each other.

104
00:11:45,891 --> 00:11:49,689
Count each column. There are 4 tuples of weight 0.

105
00:11:50,139 --> 00:11:52,251
There are 3 tuples of weight 1.

106
00:11:52,701 --> 00:11:54,706
There are 2 tuples of weight 2.

107
00:11:55,156 --> 00:11:57,481
And there is 1 tuple of weight 3.

108
00:11:57,931 --> 00:12:07,873
So phi S of x equals 4 plus 3 x plus 2 x squared plus x cubed. Check: 4 plus 3 plus 2 plus 1 is 10.

109
00:12:08,323 --> 00:12:14,488
For n tuples, weight k means that the second 1 is k plus 1 places after the first 1.

110
00:12:14,938 --> 00:12:23,919
So the first 1 can be in n minus 1 minus k places. The coefficient of x to the k is n minus 1 minus k.

111
00:12:24,369 --> 00:12:32,241
So phi of x is n minus 1, plus n minus 2 times x, and so on, up to x to the n minus 2.

112
00:12:32,691 --> 00:12:47,113
Tutorial 2, question 1 part a. S 4 is the set of tuples of length 4 with entries 1, 2 or 3. The weight is the number of places where a 1 is followed by a 1. Pause the video and try it.

113
00:12:47,563 --> 00:12:54,475
The weight depends only on where the 1s are. So first choose the positions of the 1s. They are orange.

114
00:12:54,925 --> 00:13:03,778
Every other entry is a star: 2 or 3, two choices. So a pattern with k ones gives 2 to the 4 minus k tuples.

115
00:13:04,228 --> 00:13:11,631
Weight 0. The 1s are never next to each other. No 1 at all gives 2 to the 4, which is 16 tuples.

116
00:13:12,081 --> 00:13:15,473
One 1 has 4 positions, with 8 tuples each.

117
00:13:15,923 --> 00:13:20,595
Two 1s with a gap between them: 3 patterns, with 4 tuples each.

118
00:13:21,045 --> 00:13:26,378
Weight 1. Two 1s next to each other: 3 patterns, with 4 tuples each.

119
00:13:26,828 --> 00:13:33,463
Or three 1s with one gap: 1 1 star 1, and 1 star 1 1. Each gives 2 tuples.

120
00:13:33,913 --> 00:13:39,075
Weight 2: 1 1 1 star, and star 1 1 1, with 2 tuples each.

121
00:13:39,525 --> 00:13:43,301
Weight 3: only 1 1 1 1, which is 1 tuple.

122
00:13:43,751 --> 00:13:51,986
Add the tuples in each column. Weight 0: 16, plus 4 times 8, plus 3 times 4. That is 60.

123
00:13:52,436 --> 00:13:56,959
Weight 1: 3 times 4, plus 2 times 2. That is 16.

124
00:13:57,409 --> 00:13:59,947
Weight 2: 2 times 2, which is 4.

125
00:14:00,397 --> 00:14:01,805
Weight 3: 1.

126
00:14:02,255 --> 00:14:08,591
So the generating series is 60 plus 16 x plus 4 x squared plus x cubed.

127
00:14:09,041 --> 00:14:14,332
Check: the sum is 81, and there are 3 to the 4, which is 81, tuples.

128
00:14:14,782 --> 00:14:25,171
Practice problem P2-4 part a. Now the strings have a fixed length n, and v is twice the number of 0s. Pause the video and try it.

129
00:14:25,621 --> 00:14:34,624
B n has 2 to the n elements. A finite set always has finitely many objects of each weight. So v is a weight function.

130
00:14:35,074 --> 00:14:48,685
Look at n equal to 3. The 0s are orange. The weight is twice the number of orange cells. For example, 0 1 0 has two 0s, so its weight is 2 times 2, which is 4.

131
00:14:49,135 --> 00:14:55,279
The counts are 1, 0, 3, 0, 3, 0, 1. Odd weights do not occur.

132
00:14:55,729 --> 00:15:04,475
So the series is 1 plus 3 x squared plus 3 x to the 4 plus x to the 6. That is 1 plus x squared, cubed.

133
00:15:04,925 --> 00:15:12,541
In general, choose the k positions of the 0s in n choose k ways. Each such string has weight 2 k.

134
00:15:12,991 --> 00:15:24,511
So the series is the sum of n choose k, x to the 2 k. By the Binomial Theorem with x squared in place of x, this is 1 plus x squared, to the n.

135
00:15:24,961 --> 00:15:32,513
Assignment 1, problem 4 part a. S is the set of the 16 subsets of 0, 1, 2, 3.

136
00:15:32,963 --> 00:15:35,779
The weight w is the sum of the elements.

137
00:15:36,229 --> 00:15:39,664
The weight w star is the product of the elements.

138
00:15:40,114 --> 00:15:44,615
The empty set has weight 0 for both. Pause the video and try it.

139
00:15:45,065 --> 00:15:48,777
First w. Put each subset in the column of its sum.

140
00:15:49,227 --> 00:15:55,030
The column of 3 holds the sets 3 and 1 2, and the same two sets with 0 added.

141
00:15:55,480 --> 00:16:06,445
Count the columns. The series is 2 plus 2 x plus 2 x squared plus 4 x cubed plus 2 x to the 4 plus 2 x to the 5 plus 2 x to the 6.

142
00:16:06,895 --> 00:16:21,765
Every count is even. The element 0 does not change the sum. So each subset without 0 has a partner with 0, in the same column. For example, the empty set and the set 0 both have sum 0.

143
00:16:22,215 --> 00:16:26,375
Now the product weight w star. Pause the video and try it.

144
00:16:26,825 --> 00:16:32,435
Every subset that contains 0 has product 0. There are 8 such subsets.

145
00:16:32,885 --> 00:16:37,941
The empty set also has weight 0. So 9 subsets have weight 0.

146
00:16:38,391 --> 00:16:42,765
Sort the other subsets by product. The set 1 has product 1.

147
00:16:43,215 --> 00:16:45,775
The sets 2 and 1 2 have product 2.

148
00:16:46,225 --> 00:16:48,977
The sets 3 and 1 3 have product 3.

149
00:16:49,427 --> 00:16:53,011
The sets 2 3 and 1 2 3 have product 6.

150
00:16:53,461 --> 00:16:55,871
No set has product 4 or 5.

151
00:16:56,321 --> 00:17:04,172
So the series for w star is 9 plus x plus 2 x squared plus 2 x cubed plus 2 x to the 6.

152
00:17:04,622 --> 00:17:10,211
Check: 9 plus 1 plus 2 plus 2 plus 2 is 16, the number of subsets.

153
00:17:10,661 --> 00:17:24,891
Part b. Now T is the set of all finite subsets of the natural numbers. Are the two series formal power series? That is the same as: is each weight a weight function? Pause the video and try it.

154
00:17:25,341 --> 00:17:34,791
For the sum w. Take weight n, for example n equal to 4. A set with sum 4 cannot contain an element larger than 4.

155
00:17:35,241 --> 00:17:40,319
So the set is a subset of 0 to n. That set has n plus 1 elements.

156
00:17:40,769 --> 00:17:48,961
So there are at most 2 to the n plus 1 such sets. Each T n is finite, and the series is a formal power series.

157
00:17:49,411 --> 00:18:01,443
For the product w star. Every set that contains 0 has product 0. The sets 0 1, 0 2, 0 3, and so on, all have weight 0.

158
00:18:01,893 --> 00:18:11,813
So infinitely many sets have weight 0. The constant term is not a number, and the series for w star is not a formal power series.

159
00:18:12,263 --> 00:18:28,092
Practice midterm, question 4 part a. S 3 is the set of the 7 nonempty subsets of 1, 2, 3. The weight w is the smallest element. The weight v is the largest element. Pause the video and try it.

160
00:18:28,542 --> 00:18:33,918
Sort by the smallest element. Four sets contain 1, so they have minimum 1.

161
00:18:34,368 --> 00:18:36,949
The sets 2 and 2 3 have minimum 2.

162
00:18:37,399 --> 00:18:39,789
Only the set 3 has minimum 3.

163
00:18:40,239 --> 00:18:45,124
Now sort by the largest element. Only the set 1 has maximum 1.

164
00:18:45,574 --> 00:18:48,241
The sets 2 and 1 2 have maximum 2.

165
00:18:48,691 --> 00:18:50,803
Four sets have maximum 3.

166
00:18:51,253 --> 00:18:55,711
So phi w is 4 x plus 2 x squared plus x cubed.

167
00:18:56,161 --> 00:19:00,193
And phi v is x plus 2 x squared plus 4 x cubed.

168
00:19:00,643 --> 00:19:08,814
In general, minimum k leaves the n minus k larger elements free. So there are 2 to the n minus k sets.

169
00:19:09,264 --> 00:19:16,539
Maximum k leaves the k minus 1 smaller elements free. So there are 2 to the k minus 1 sets.

170
00:19:16,989 --> 00:19:23,986
Part b. T is now the set of finite subsets of the natural numbers. Pause the video and try it.

171
00:19:24,436 --> 00:19:29,791
The empty set has no minimum or maximum, so we use the nonempty subsets.

172
00:19:30,241 --> 00:19:37,793
For the minimum. The sets 2, 2 3, 2 4, 2 3 4, 2 5, and so on, all have minimum 2.

173
00:19:38,243 --> 00:19:47,907
There is no upper limit on the other elements. So infinitely many sets have weight 2, and the series for w is not a formal power series.

174
00:19:48,357 --> 00:20:02,223
For the maximum. A set with maximum n is n, together with any subset Y of 0 to n minus 1. For example, for n equal to 4, Y is a subset of 0, 1, 2, 3.

175
00:20:02,673 --> 00:20:10,801
There are n numbers from 0 to n minus 1. So there are 2 to the n choices for Y, and each T n is finite.

176
00:20:11,251 --> 00:20:17,459
So v is a weight function, and the series for v is the sum of 2 to the n, x to the n.

177
00:20:17,909 --> 00:20:24,416
By the geometric series with 2 x in place of x, this is 1 over 1 minus 2 x.

178
00:20:24,866 --> 00:20:39,693
Practice midterm, question 5. S is the set of the numbers 0 to 50. The weight of a is 4 a plus 3. Find the generating series as a simplified rational expression. Pause the video and try it.

179
00:20:40,143 --> 00:20:49,401
Each a gives one term, x to the 4 a plus 3. So 0 gives x to the 4 times 0 plus 3, which is x cubed.

180
00:20:49,851 --> 00:20:53,990
1 gives x to the 4 plus 3, which is x to the 7.

181
00:20:54,440 --> 00:21:00,499
And 50 gives x to the 4 times 50 plus 3, which is x to the 203.

182
00:21:00,949 --> 00:21:14,965
As a row of coefficients, there is a 1 at positions 3, 7, 11, 15, and so on. All other coefficients are 0. Each weight occurs at most once, so w is a weight function.

183
00:21:15,415 --> 00:21:21,111
Write the definition: the sum over a from 0 to 50 of x to the 4 a plus 3.

184
00:21:21,561 --> 00:21:28,793
Take out x cubed. What is left is a finite geometric sum in x to the 4, with 51 terms.

185
00:21:29,243 --> 00:21:39,419
The sum of r to the a, for a from 0 to 50, is 1 minus r to the 51, over 1 minus r. Here r is x to the 4.

186
00:21:39,869 --> 00:21:50,642
4 times 51 is 204. So the result is x cubed times 1 minus x to the 204, over 1 minus x to the 4.

187
00:21:51,092 --> 00:22:01,033
Practice problem P2-3. We know the generating series, and we want the number of elements of weight 15. Pause the video and try it.

188
00:22:01,483 --> 00:22:07,094
By Proposition 2.7, the answer is the coefficient of x to the 15.

189
00:22:07,544 --> 00:22:15,245
x over 1 minus 2 x, to the 7, is x to the 7 times 1 minus 2 x, to the minus 7.

190
00:22:15,695 --> 00:22:26,938
The factor x to the 7 moves every coefficient 7 places. So we need the coefficient of x to the 15 minus 7, which is x to the 8, in the rest.

191
00:22:27,388 --> 00:22:42,151
Call c k the coefficient of x to the k in 1 minus 2 x, to the minus 7. The Binomial Series with 2 x in place of x gives c k equals k plus 6 choose 6, times 2 to the k.

192
00:22:42,601 --> 00:22:46,974
Multiply by 1 plus x. The 1 gives the row of c k.

193
00:22:47,424 --> 00:22:50,795
The x gives the same row, moved one place right.

194
00:22:51,245 --> 00:22:54,765
The column of x to the 8 holds c 8 and c 7.

195
00:22:55,215 --> 00:23:09,444
So the answer is c 8 plus c 7. That is 14 choose 6 times 2 to the 8, plus 13 choose 6 times 2 to the 7. This is the posted answer. You do not need to evaluate it.

196
00:23:09,894 --> 00:23:16,763
On a test, you must justify each answer. A full answer has four parts. First, define the set.

197
00:23:17,213 --> 00:23:20,904
Second, define the weight, and check that it is a weight function.

198
00:23:21,354 --> 00:23:26,239
Third, find the generating series, and name each result that you use.

199
00:23:26,689 --> 00:23:28,567
Fourth, read the coefficient.

200
00:23:29,017 --> 00:23:34,137
Here is a full answer to P1-8. It defines S and w.

201
00:23:34,587 --> 00:23:37,125
It says why w is a weight function.

202
00:23:37,575 --> 00:23:40,775
It gives the counts of each weight, and then the series.

203
00:23:41,225 --> 00:23:52,489
When you count with a series, say why the coefficient is the answer: by Proposition 2.7. The practice midterm asks you to name each result that you use.

204
00:23:52,939 --> 00:24:00,641
To recap. A weight function gives each object a natural number, with only finitely many objects of each weight.

205
00:24:01,091 --> 00:24:05,741
If one weight has infinitely many objects, there is no power series.

206
00:24:06,191 --> 00:24:10,693
The generating series has one term x to the weight for each object.

207
00:24:11,143 --> 00:24:18,183
By Proposition 2.7, the coefficient of x to the n is the number of objects of weight n.

208
00:24:18,633 --> 00:24:26,867
For a small set, list the objects and collect the terms. For a large set, count the objects of weight n in general.

209
00:24:27,317 --> 00:24:35,488
The next video gives the Sum, Product and String Lemmas. They build the series of a large set from the series of smaller sets.
